A **pictograph** uses a symbol to stand for a number — read the **key** first.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\node[right] at (0,2.6) {Group A}; \node[right] at (0,1.7) {Group B};
\foreach \x in {2.8,3.5,4.2,4.9} {\fill (\x,2.6) circle (4pt);}
\foreach \x in {2.8,3.5,4.2} {\fill (\x,1.7) circle (4pt);}
\fill (2.8,0.4) circle (4pt); \node[right] at (3.1,0.4) {$=n$ items (the key)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
A wildlife website shows the animals released this week.

a. How many birds were released?
b. Which animal group had the fewest releases, and how many?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node at (4,4) {Animals released this week};
\node[anchor=east] at (2,3) {Birds};
\node[anchor=east] at (2,2) {Reptiles};
\node[anchor=east] at (2,1) {Mammals};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6,5.3,6} {\draw (\x,3) circle (2mm);}
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9} {\draw (\x,2) circle (2mm);}
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6} {\draw (\x,1) circle (2mm);}
\draw (2.5,0) circle (2mm);
\node[anchor=west] at (2.9,0) {represents 4 animals};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two rescue newsletters show the animals treated. How many more animals did Centre B treat than Centre A?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node[anchor=east] at (2,3) {Centre A};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6} {\draw (\x,3) circle (2mm);}
\draw (2.5,2.3) circle (2mm);
\node[anchor=west] at (2.9,2.3) {represents 5 animals};
\node[anchor=east] at (2,1.2) {Centre B};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9} {\draw (\x,1.2) circle (2mm);}
\draw (2.5,0.5) circle (2mm);
\node[anchor=west] at (2.9,0.5) {represents 8 animals};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A rescue centre's webpage shows the kangaroos in its care. The bar has $8$ equal sections. What fraction of the kangaroos are joeys?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node at (4,2.3) {Kangaroos in care};
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,0.8);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,0.8);}
\draw[very thick] (5,0)--(5,0.8);
\draw (0,1)--(0,1.15)--(5,1.15)--(5,1);
\draw (5.1,1)--(5.1,1.15)--(8,1.15)--(8,1);
\node at (2.5,1.55) {Joeys};
\node at (6.55,1.55) {Adults};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A social-media post claims, ‘At least $40$ turtles released!’ Does its pictograph support the claim? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node at (3.5,2) {Turtles released};
\foreach \x in {1,1.8,2.6,3.4,4.2,5} {\draw (\x,1) circle (2mm);}
\draw (1,0) circle (2mm);
\node[anchor=west] at (1.4,0) {represents 6 turtles};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An infographic shows $5$ identical symbols for a total of $30$ wildlife checks. Its second row uses the same key. How many checks does the second row represent?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node[anchor=east] at (2,2) {First row};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6,5.3} {\draw (\x,2) circle (2mm);}
\node[anchor=west] at (5.7,2) {30 checks};
\node[anchor=east] at (2,1) {Second row};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6,5.3,6,6.7} {\draw (\x,1) circle (2mm);}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A wildlife-rescue website uses the pictograph below.
How many possums were rescued?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\node at (3.5,3.8) {Animals rescued this week};
\node[right] at (0,2.8) {Possums}; \node[right] at (0,1.9) {Echidnas}; \node[right] at (0,1.0) {Wallabies};
\foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6,5.3} {\fill (\x,2.8) circle (4pt);} \foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9} {\fill (\x,1.9) circle (4pt);} \foreach \x in {2.5,3.2,3.9,4.6} {\fill (\x,1.0) circle (4pt);}
\fill (2.5,0) circle (4pt); \node[right] at (2.8,0) {$=4$ animals};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A park newsletter uses this pictograph.
How many more koalas than wombats were sighted?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\node at (3.6,3.2) {Ranger sightings}; \node[right] at (0,2.1) {Koalas}; \node[right] at (0,1.2) {Wombats};
\foreach \x in {2.3,3.0,3.7,4.4,5.1,5.8} {\fill (\x,2.1) circle (4pt);} \foreach \x in {2.3,3.0,3.7,4.4} {\fill (\x,1.2) circle (4pt);}
\fill (2.3,0.2) circle (4pt); \node[right] at (2.6,0.2) {$=3$ sightings};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A rescue poster shows the weekly feed bags used.
How many bags were used for flying foxes?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\node at (3.6,3.2) {Feed bags used}; \node[right] at (0,2.1) {Gliders}; \node[right] at (0,1.2) {Flying foxes};
\foreach \x in {2.7,3.4,4.1,4.8} {\draw (\x,2.1) circle (5pt);} \foreach \x in {2.7,3.4,4.1,4.8,5.5} {\draw (\x,1.2) circle (5pt);}
\draw (2.7,0.2) circle (5pt); \node[right] at (3,0.2) {$=3$ bags};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

